            $q('What problems can concurrent transactions cause, and which isolation levels prevent them?',
                'Dirty read, non-repeatable read, phantom read and lost update. READ UNCOMMITTED allows all; READ COMMITTED prevents dirty reads; REPEATABLE READ prevents non-repeatable reads; SERIALIZABLE prevents phantoms too, at the cost of concurrency.', 'Concurrency'),
            $q('Explain two-phase locking. Does it guarantee that deadlocks cannot happen?',
                'Growing phase acquires locks, shrinking phase releases and no new lock after the first release; this guarantees conflict serializability. It does not prevent deadlock; the DBMS needs detection (wait-for graph) or prevention (wait-die, wound-wait, timeouts).', 'Concurrency'),
            $q('What is the difference between a clustered and a non-clustered index? How many of each can a table have?',
                'A clustered index defines the physical order of the rows, so one per table; the leaf level is the data. A non-clustered index is a separate structure with pointers to the rows; many allowed. Range queries on the clustered key are fastest.', 'Indexing'),
        ],
        3 => [
            $q('A query on a large table has become slow. How do you investigate and fix it?',
                'Use EXPLAIN / the execution plan to find full scans, check for missing or unused indexes, selective predicates, avoid functions on indexed columns and SELECT *, check join order and statistics, consider covering indexes, pagination, caching or denormalization if justified, and measure after each change.', 'Query tuning', 180),
            $q('How does a B+ tree index work, and why do databases prefer it to a binary search tree or a hash index?',
